course: world
lesson: 164
title: "It worked once": one example is not all
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Little Finn jumped in a puddle once and didn't get told off. Now he jumps in every puddle: "It was fine last time!" But last time was a Saturday in wellies, and today he's in his school uniform. One time told him almost nothing.
explain: Turning one example into a rule is called a hasty generalisation. One dog bit a postman, so "all dogs bite postmen"; one cake burned, so "I'm useless at baking, forever"; one parking spot at 5 o'clock, so it's "the magic time".
explain: A cousin of the trap is the lucky survivor: Gran's neighbour smoked and lived to 95, so "smoking can't be that bad". Doctors have counted millions of people, and on average smoking makes people ill and die much earlier. One lucky person doesn't change the count.
explain: Another cousin: getting better anyway. Uncle Pat's cold went after a hot drink, but colds go by themselves in about a week. Chloe hid behind the sofa in a storm and was fine, but the whole house kept her safe.
explain: The mickey-taking hits the jump from one to all: "Nana, I put honey on my maths. Now it's sticky AND wrong." To really know, try many times, change one thing, and count.
fact: Making a rule from one example is called a hasty generalisation: one time tells you very little.
fact: Ask what was different last time: the same action in a different situation can give a different result.
fact: Some smokers live long, but doctors have counted huge numbers of people, and on average smoking makes people ill and die much earlier.
fact: Pointing to one lucky person against the evidence is sometimes called the survivor story.
fact: Most colds go away by themselves in about 7 to 10 days, which is why many "cures" seem to work; whiskey is never for children.
fact: Honey can soothe a cough a little in children over one year old, but it is not a cure for everything.
fact: Dogs differ a lot: one dog's behaviour tells you about that dog, not about all dogs.
fact: Inside a house you are already safe from lightning; staying away from windows, baths and corded phones in a storm also helps.
fact: One bad try at something new is normal; change one thing, like the oven temperature, and try again.
fact: Car parks are usually busiest at weekends and in the evening rush; one lucky spot at 5 o'clock proves nothing.
fact: In Monopoly the brown streets are the cheapest and earn the least rent; a strategy is tested over many games, not one.
fact: Words people use: "eejit" means idiot and "ya" means you (Ireland), "cos" means because, "mid" means ordinary or not very good (young slang), "fam" means mate, "bummer" means a disappointment (older US slang), "muppet" is a teasing word for a silly person (UK), and "hot whiskey" is a grown-up drink in Ireland.
exercise: Finn jumped in a puddle once without being told off. Why is it different today? | He is in his school uniform, not wellies on a Saturday | Puddles are deeper on weekdays | Mum has stopped liking puddles | It is a different puddle from last time, and much muddier than before
exercise: Gran says her neighbour smoked and lived to 95, so smoking "can't be that bad". What is wrong? | One lucky person doesn't beat evidence from millions | Her neighbour must have stopped smoking | Smoking is fine if you live in the countryside | 95 is not really very old
exercise: What does counting huge numbers of people show about smoking? | On average smokers get ill and die much earlier | Smokers live just as long as everyone else | Smoking only harms old people | Smoking is safe for the first 50 years, and only harms you after that
exercise: Josh ate his sister's chocolate once without being caught and says, "I never get caught." What happens? | He leaves the wrapper on his bed the second time | He never gets caught again | His sister eats his chocolate instead | He blames the dog for it and gets away with it every single time
exercise: Mum burned one new recipe and says she's useless at baking forever. What is the trap? | One bad try is turned into "always bad" | She should never have tried baking at all | New recipes always burn the very first time | She is right, some people just can't bake
exercise: What was the likely reason Mum's cake burned? | The oven was too hot | Mum is bad at everything | The recipe was cursed | The cake tin was the wrong colour
exercise: Uncle Pat in Cork says hot whiskey cured his cold. What really happened? | Colds go away by themselves in about a week | Whiskey kills cold germs | His cold moved to someone else | The drink worked because it was hot, so any hot drink cures a cold
exercise: Harper's friend says all dogs bite postmen because one dog did. What is true? | Dogs differ; one dog tells you about that dog only | All dogs hate postmen's uniforms | Only small dogs bite postmen | Dogs bite postmen because the letters smell of other people's dogs
exercise: Dad found a parking spot at 5 o'clock once and calls it "the magic time". How could he really find out? | Try many days at 5 and at other times, and count | Go at exactly 5 o'clock again tomorrow | Ask the shop to save his spot | Trust it, because it worked once and that's proof enough for anyone
exercise: Nana says honey cures everything because it helped Alfie's cough. What is true about honey? | It can soothe a cough a little but cures nothing else here | It cures scraped knees and sad moods | It is better than any medicine | It helps with maths if you eat a spoonful of it before you start
exercise: Chloe says the sofa protects from lightning. What really keeps her safe? | Being inside the house | The cushions on the sofa | Hiding with her eyes shut | Being close to the floor
exercise: Max won Monopoly once with the brown streets, then lost five games. What is true about the brown streets? | They are the cheapest and earn the least rent | They are the best streets in the game | They win if you buy them first | They are lucky for the youngest player, so Max should always pick them
exercise: What do grown-ups call making a rule from one example? | A hasty generalisation | A fair test | A strong argument | An appeal to authority
exercise: How long do most colds last without any cure? | About 7 to 10 days | About one hour | About three months | Forever, unless you take a cure
exercise: Which is the best way to test a strategy in a game? | Play it over many games and count the wins | Play it just once, and see whether you win or not | Ask a friend whether the plan sounds any good to them | Copy whoever happened to win the game last time
test: Mum: "Last time you were in wellies on a Saturday, Finn. Today you're in your school trousers, you absolute muppet." Who or what is she mocking? | The idea that last time proves today, not Finn himself | Finn's school trousers | People who wear wellies | Saturdays
test: Kai: "Gran, one person won the lottery, doesn't mean we'll all win the lottery." Why is this a good comeback? | It shows that one lucky case isn't the usual result | It proves smoking is good for you | It means Gran should buy a lottery ticket every single week from now on | It shows Kai likes the lottery
test: Josh's sister: "Never get caught? The wrapper's on your pillow, genius." What is the real lesson here? | One time tells you little, and don't eat other people's chocolate | Hide wrappers better next time | Always eat chocolate in your own room | Sisters never notice missing chocolate, so next time it'll be fine to eat it
test: Daughter: "Mum, it was the oven being dramatic, not you being useless." What should Mum do? | Change the temperature and try again | Never bake again | Buy a new cake tin | Blame the recipe book
test: Pat's sister: "Your cold went cos it was day seven, ya eejit." What is she saying? | The cold would have gone anyway, without the drink | The whiskey made the cold go away a lot faster than usual | Colds always last exactly seven days, never more or less | Pat caught the cold from his sister in the first place
test: Harper: "My nan's dog licked the postman so much he had to change his shirt. So no, not all dogs, fam." What does her example do? | It shows one case can break an "all" rule | It proves all dogs lick postmen | It shows postmen don't like dogs | It proves her nan's dog is the only friendly one
test: Teen: "Dad, the magic time was a fluke. It's Saturday, the whole town's here. Bummer." What is a fluke? | A lucky one-off that happened by chance | A rule that always works | A type of parking ticket | The busiest time of day
test: Alfie: "Nana, I put honey on my maths. Now it's sticky AND wrong." What trap is he laughing at? | Stretching one success to everything | Using too little honey | Doing maths in the kitchen | Thinking honey helps coughs
test: What would happen if Chloe hid behind the sofa in a field during a storm? | She would not be safe; it was the house that protected her | She would be safe because sofas stop lightning | She would be safer than inside a house | Lightning only ever strikes sofas that are left outdoors in fields
test: Mum: "You've lost five in a row, Max. Your strategy is mid, love." What does she mean? | His plan isn't good; that one win was luck | His plan is brilliant but unlucky | He should buy more brown streets | Five losses in a row prove that the whole game must be broken
test: In a film, a boy eats a funny mushroom once, feels fine, and says "all wild mushrooms are safe". Put it right. | Some wild mushrooms are deadly; one safe one proves nothing | All wild mushrooms are safe to eat | Only red mushrooms are dangerous | Cooking makes every mushroom safe
exam: Identify the principal error in concluding, from a single neighbour who smoked and attained the age of ninety-five, that smoking is not harmful. | A single fortunate case is set against evidence drawn from very large populations | The neighbour's age was insufficiently advanced | The neighbour must have consumed honey regularly | Smoking was less common in former times
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the duration of a common cold in the absence of treatment. | It ordinarily resolves within approximately seven to ten days | It ordinarily persists for several months | It never resolves without a glass of hot whiskey taken at bedtime | It resolves within approximately one hour
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the derivation of a universal rule from one instance? | A hasty generalisation | A controlled experiment | A survey of many cases | A definition
exam: Determine what principally protects a person from lightning when sheltering behind a sofa during a thunderstorm. | The surrounding house | The upholstery of the sofa | The proximity of the floor | The closing of the eyes
